+++
title = "Image Processing"
template = "docs"
+++

Revela automatically processes your images into optimized web formats. This page covers configuration, incremental updates, and cleanup.

## Image Configuration

Configure image formats and quality in `project.json`, or with `revela config image`:

```json
{
  "generate": {
    "images": {
      "avif": 80,
      "webp": 85,
      "jpg": 90
    }
  }
}
```

**Supported formats:**
- `jpg` - Universal compatibility (quality 1-100)
- `webp` - Smaller files, modern browsers (quality 1-100)
- `avif` - Smallest files, newest browsers (quality 1-100)

Set a format to `0` (or leave it out) to disable it. The project wizard enables all three with the values above, and they are a good default: browsers pick the smallest format they support, and JPG remains the fallback. If builds take too long, see [Encoding Speed](#encoding-speed) before disabling AVIF, which is the slowest to encode.

```bash
revela config image --formats avif:80,webp:85,jpg:90
```

## Image Sizes

Sizes are defined by your theme in its `Configuration/images.json`. The default Lumina theme generates these widths:

```
160, 320, 480, 640, 720, 960, 1280, 1440, 1920, 2560
```

These sizes are optimized for High-DPI displays (1.5x, 2x scaling).

Each photo gets every theme size that is **smaller than the photo**, plus its **original width** for the full-resolution view. A 2000-pixel-wide photo, for example, gets 160 to 1920 and 2000. To limit that largest image, see [`maxSize`](#limit-the-largest-image-maxsize).

To use different sizes, see [Themes](../themes/#theme-structure).

## Color Spaces

Generated images are always **sRGB**, the color space browsers assume for web images.
Photos exported with another embedded profile (Display P3, Adobe RGB, CMYK) are converted
using that profile, so colors look the same as in your editor on standard displays.

Colors that sRGB cannot represent — very saturated reds, greens and cyans that wide-gamut
displays can show — are mapped to the closest sRGB color. Your source files are never
changed. For full control over that mapping, soft-proof and export in sRGB from your
photo editor.

HDR photos (JPEG with gain map) are published as their standard dynamic range image.

## Detail View Zoom

Canonical photo pages and Lightbox images can be zoomed:

- **Mouse or pen:** click to zoom to 100 % (one image pixel per screen pixel, also on high-density
  screens), drag to move around, click again to zoom out.
- **Touch:** double-tap to zoom to 100 % or back, pinch with two fingers, and move with one finger
  while zoomed. A pinch past 100 % or below the fitted size springs back when you let go.

Zoom stops at 100 % because beyond it a photo only shows enlarged pixels. Photos that already fit
the screen at 100 % or less are not zoomable and show no zoom cursor. With
[`maxSize`](#limit-the-largest-image-maxsize), the largest image is the capped one, so zooming
shows no more detail than that.

The page first loads a photo that fits the screen and fetches the original only when someone
zooms; until it has arrived the current photo is shown enlarged, so it never goes blank. Zoom
resets when a Lightbox closes or switches images.

A Lightbox fills the whole screen and closes with its × button or the Escape key; clicking
around the photo does not close it.

Mouse-wheel zoom and swipe-to-close are intentionally not enabled, so vertical scrolling to
photo metadata remains predictable.

## Generating Images

```bash
# Full generation
revela generate images

# Force regenerate all (ignore cache)
revela generate images --force
```

## Incremental Processing

Revela automatically detects what's missing and only generates those variants.
An image is regenerated completely when its source file changes (size or
modification time), when the theme's resize mode changes, or when a Revela
update changes how images are encoded. Revela remembers what each photo's
variants were made from in `.cache/images.json`, separately from the scan data,
so a rescan or a Revela update that only changes the scan data never re-encodes
your photos. Deleting `.cache/` (`revela clean cache`) forgets it, and the next
build re-encodes every photo.

Large libraries can take a while, especially the first time or after such an
update. If a run is interrupted (Ctrl+C, a closed terminal or a restart), just
start it again: Revela saves its progress as it goes and continues with the
photos that weren't finished yet.

### Adding a New Format

```bash
# Before: only jpg configured
# After: add webp to project.json

revela generate images
# → Only webp files are created
# → Existing jpg files are kept
```

### Changing Quality

When you change a format's quality (for example AVIF from 80 to 70), the next build re-encodes **only that format** for all photos. The other formats are kept, and you don't need `--force`. Changing `avifEffort` or `webpEffort` works the same way, and changing `maxSize` re-encodes only the photos larger than the old or the new limit (see [Encoding Speed](#encoding-speed)).

### Progress Display

While images are encoded, Revela shows one progress bar for all photos and a status line below it:

```
Encoding photos  ████████████░░░░░░░░  512/1197 (43%)  ·  18m elapsed  ·  ~24m left
⠋ 16 workers busy · avif 512 · webp 998 · jpg 1120 · 132 img/min · 3 skipped
```

The status line shows how many photos are finished per format, the processing rate, and how many photos were skipped because they were already up to date.

## Encoding Speed

Almost all of the build time goes into encoding, and AVIF takes most of it: for a 25-megapixel
photo with AVIF 80, WebP 85 and JPG 90, AVIF is over 90 % of the work, and the full-resolution
image alone about half of the AVIF time. Two optional settings trade some file size or zoom
detail for much faster builds; by default both are off.

| Setting (`generate.images`) | Default | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| `avifEffort` | `4` | AVIF encoder effort, `0` (fastest) to `9` (smallest files) |
| `webpEffort` | `4` | WebP encoder effort, `0` to `6` |
| `maxSize` | `0` (off) | Largest image in pixels, measured like the theme sizes (the longest edge in Lumina) |
| `maxDegreeOfParallelism` | automatic | Photos encoded at the same time |

### Faster AVIF (`avifEffort`)

```json
{
  "generate": {
    "images": {
      "avif": 80,
      "avifEffort": 2
    }
  }
}
```

Measured on 25-megapixel photos, effort `2` encodes AVIF about 8× faster, so a photo with all
three formats needs about **3.5× less CPU time**. The AVIF files are **4–11 % larger** (the more
detail and the larger the image, the more), and the quality is about the same. On a small
server this turns hours into minutes. `webpEffort` works the same way for WebP; WebP is far
less of the build time, so it matters less.

Changing an effort re-encodes only that format's files, like a quality change.

### Limit the Largest Image (`maxSize`)

```json
{
  "generate": {
    "images": {
      "maxSize": 3840
    }
  }
}
```

Every photo normally also gets its full resolution, so the photo viewer can zoom to 100 %. With
`maxSize`, photos larger than the limit get an image of that size instead; smaller photos keep
their full resolution. `3840` (4K) needs about **25–35 % less time** per photo (more for larger
photos), and the files of the largest image shrink accordingly, but zooming shows no more detail
than 3840 pixels. Together with `avifEffort: 2`, a photo needs about 80 % less time than with
the defaults.

Changing `maxSize` re-encodes the photos larger than the old or the new limit. The
full-resolution files they had before are no longer used; `revela clean images` removes them.

### Parallel Encoding

Revela encodes several photos at the same time. Without AVIF it encodes about one photo per CPU
core, each with one or two threads, because the JPEG and WebP encoders use a single thread. With
AVIF it encodes half as many photos as there are cores with up to 8 threads each, which suits the
AVIF encoder best.

Each photo being encoded needs up to about 700 MB of memory (a full-resolution AVIF of a
30-megapixel photo), so Revela encodes at most one photo per GB of memory. To use less memory,
set `maxDegreeOfParallelism` to a smaller number, for example `1` to encode one photo at a time.
Changing it never re-encodes photos.

## Cleaning Up

When you change your image configuration (remove a format, change sizes), old files remain. Use `clean images` to remove them:

```bash
# Preview what would be deleted
revela clean images --dry-run

# Actually delete unused files
revela clean images
```

### What Gets Cleaned

| Category | Description | Example |
|----------|-------------|---------|
| **Orphaned folders** | Source image was deleted | `output/images/galleries/vacation/deleted-photo/` |
| **Unused sizes** | Width no longer in theme config | `output/images/<gallery>/<photo>/480.jpg` when 480 removed |
| **Unused formats** | Format disabled in project | `output/images/<gallery>/<photo>/*.webp` when webp removed |

> **Scan first.** `clean images` compares the output with the manifest. `revela generate all` (or `revela generate scan`) brings the manifest up to date, so run it before cleaning.

### Typical Workflow

```bash
# 1. Change config (remove webp)
# Edit project.json: remove "webp": 85

# 2. Preview cleanup
revela clean images --dry-run
# → Shows: 4220 webp files (852 MB)

# 3. Delete unused files
revela clean images
# → Deleted: 4220 files (852 MB)

# 4. Verify (optional)
revela generate images
# → Shows: 0 processed, 1153 cached
```

## Low-Quality Image Placeholders (LQIP)

Revela generates **CSS-only placeholders** that display while images load. This creates a smooth loading experience without layout shifts.

### How It Works

1. During scan, Revela analyzes the average color and brightness pattern of a small preview of each image (the full-size photo is not decoded)
2. This data is encoded into a compact integer (e.g., `-721311`)
3. The theme's CSS decodes this integer into gradient placeholders
4. When the image loads, it smoothly replaces the placeholder

**Result:** ~7 bytes per image instead of a separate LQIP file. No additional HTTP requests.

### Configuration

LQIP is **enabled by default**. To disable it:

```json
{
  "generate": {
    "images": {
      "placeholder": {
        "strategy": "none"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

### Technical Details

The CSS hash encodes:
- **Average color** in Oklab color space (8 bits)
- **3×2 brightness grid** as grayscale values (12 bits)

The browser reconstructs this as radial gradients with blend modes, creating a blurred approximation of the image. Based on [Lean Rada's CSS-only LQIP technique](https://leanrada.com/notes/css-only-lqip/).

## Best Practices

<div class="features-grid">

### 🚀 Keep the Defaults

AVIF 80, WebP 85 and JPG 90 give small files with a safe fallback. If encoding takes too long, lower `avifEffort` or set `maxSize` before you disable AVIF.

### 🔄 Incremental Updates

Don't use `--force` unless necessary. Incremental processing is much faster, and quality changes are picked up automatically.

### 🧹 Clean After Config Changes

After removing formats or sizes, run `clean images` to free disk space.

### 📊 Check Dry-Run First

Always use `--dry-run` before `clean images` to preview what will be deleted.

</div>

## Troubleshooting

If images are missing or `generate images` fails, run `revela check` first. It reports problems such as a missing source folder, an output folder Revela can't write to, a missing theme or invalid settings, each with a suggested fix.
